@subsection Implementing the Query System

Section @ref{4.4.2} described how the query system works. Now we fill in the
details by presenting a complete implementation of the system.  Terms, queries,
rules, and frames are ordinary OCaml variants and lists, built by the
constructors below.  There is no query text to parse: a session is a list of
commands, and the fixture reader of @ref{4.4.4.7} decodes the same commands
from constructor data.

@menu
* 4.4.4.1::          The Driver Loop and Instantiation
* 4.4.4.2::          The Evaluator
* 4.4.4.3::          Finding Assertions by Pattern Matching
* 4.4.4.4::          Rules and Unification
* 4.4.4.5::          Maintaining the Data Base
* 4.4.4.6::          Stream Operations
* 4.4.4.7::          Query Syntax Procedures
* 4.4.4.8::          Frames and Bindings
@end menu

@node 4.4.4.1, 4.4.4.2, 4.4.4, 4.4.4
@subsubsection The Driver Loop and Instantiation

A term is an atom, a number, a string, a variable, the empty list, or a pair.
A query is a pattern, a conjunction, a disjunction, a negation, a host
predicate application, the always-true query, or a special form dispatched by
name.  A command files an assertion, files a rule, or asks a query.  A frame
is an association list of variables to terms, newest first:

@example ocaml
type variable =
  @{ name : string
  ; id : int
  @}

type term =
  | Atom of string
  | Num of int
  | Str of string
  | Var of variable
  | Nil
  | Pair of term * term

type query =
  | Pattern of term
  | And of query list
  | Or of query list
  | Not of query
  | Holds of string * term list
  | Always_true
  | Form of string * query list

type command =
  | Assert of term
  | Rule of term * query
  | Query of query

type frame = (variable * term) list

exception Query_error of Eval_error.t
@end example

@noindent
The @code{id} of a variable is the rule application that renamed it, and
@code{0} for a variable of the query itself.  Lists are @code{Pair} structure
ending in @code{Nil}, so a rule may match a list's head and its tail
separately.  Three smart constructors build the shapes the sessions use:

@example ocaml
let list items =
  List.fold_right (fun head tail -> Pair (head, tail)) items Nil
;;

let dotted items tail =
  List.fold_right (fun head tail -> Pair (head, tail)) items tail
;;

let var name = Var @{ name; id = 0 @}
@end example

@noindent
The driver @code{run} files the assertions and rules of its commands in order,
and for each query prints the query prefixed by @code{?} and then one line per
answer.  Answers are instantiations of the query by its answer frames;
variables the frames leave unbound print with the query's own names, and
renamed rule variables print with numbered suffixes:

@example ocaml
let run ~emit session commands =
  let rec each s =
    match s with
    | Streams.Empty -> ()
    | Streams.Cons (answer, tail) ->
      emit (render_query (canonical answer) ^ "\n");
      each (Lazy.force tail)
  in
  let command = function
    | Assert t -> Ok (add_assertion session t)
    | Rule (conclusion, body) -> Ok (add_rule session conclusion body)
    | Query q ->
      emit ("? " ^ render_query q ^ "\n");
      let* s = answers session q in
      (match each s with
       | () -> Ok ()
       | exception Query_error e -> Error e)
  in
  List.fold_left
    (fun acc c -> Result.bind acc (fun () -> command c))
    (Ok ())
    commands
;;
@end example

@noindent
An answer's unbound rule variables are numbered by first occurrence within the
answer, so the printed form does not depend on how many rule applications the
search happened to make before it.  A failure inside lazy answer production,
such as a @code{Holds} with an unbound variable or an unknown special form,
raises @code{Query_error}.

To instantiate a term, we copy it, replacing any variables in the term by
their values in a given frame.  The values are themselves instantiated, since
they could contain variables (for example, if @code{?x} is bound to
@code{?y} as the result of unification and @code{?y} is in turn bound to a
term).  The action to take if a variable cannot be instantiated is given by a
procedural argument to @code{instantiate}:

@example ocaml
let rec instantiate t frame unbound =
  match t with
  | Var v ->
    (match binding_in_frame v frame with
     | Some bound -> instantiate bound frame unbound
     | None -> unbound v)
  | Pair (a, b) ->
    Pair (instantiate a frame unbound, instantiate b frame unbound)
  | Atom _ | Num _ | Str _ | Nil -> t
;;
@end example

@noindent
The procedures that manipulate bindings are defined in @ref{4.4.4.8}.

@node 4.4.4.2, 4.4.4.3, 4.4.4.1, 4.4.4
@subsubsection The Evaluator

The @code{qeval} procedure, called by @code{run}, is the basic evaluator of
the query system.  It takes as inputs the session, a query, and a stream of
frames, and it returns a stream of extended frames.  It classifies the query
by matching on its constructor.  @code{Form} queries dispatch through the
session's handler table, installed by @code{put}, as the book's @code{put}
does.  Any @code{Pattern} query is assumed to be a simple query, to be
processed by @code{simple_query}:

@example ocaml
let rec qeval session q frames =
  match q with
  | Pattern p ->
    stream_flatmap (fun frame -> simple_query session p frame) frames
  | And conjuncts ->
    List.fold_left
      (fun frames c -> qeval session c frames)
      frames conjuncts
  | Or disjuncts -> disjoin session disjuncts frames
  | Not inner ->
    Streams.stream_filter
      (fun frame ->
        Streams.stream_null
          (qeval session inner (singleton_stream frame)))
      frames
  | Holds (name, args) ->
    Streams.stream_filter
      (fun frame ->
        predicate
          name
          (List.map
             (fun t ->
               instantiate t frame (fun v ->
                 fail
                   (Eval_error.Unbound_variable
                      ("holds on unbound "
                       ^ render_variable v))))
             args))
      frames
  | Always_true -> frames
  | Form (name, operands) ->
    (match Hashtbl.find_opt session.handlers name with
     | Some handler -> handler session operands frames
     | None ->
       fail (Eval_error.Invalid_form ("unknown query form " ^ name)))
@end example

@subsubheading Simple queries

The @code{simple_query} procedure handles simple queries.  It takes as
arguments a simple query (a pattern) together with one frame, and it returns
the stream formed by extending the frame by all data-base matches of the
query.

@example ocaml
and simple_query session pattern frame =
  stream_append_delayed
    (find_assertions session pattern frame)
    (fun () -> apply_rules session pattern frame)
@end example

@noindent
For the frame we use @code{find_assertions}
(@ref{4.4.4.3}) to match the pattern against all assertions in the data base,
producing a stream of extended frames, and we use @code{apply_rules}
(@ref{4.4.4.4}) to apply all possible rules, producing another stream of
extended frames.  These two streams are combined (using
@code{stream_append_delayed}, @ref{4.4.4.6}) to make a stream of all
the ways that the given pattern can be satisfied consistent with the original
frame (see @ref{Exercise 4.71}).  Assertion matches come before rule-derived
answers, in assertion order, because the assertion stream is the first
argument.  The streams for the individual input frames
are combined using @code{stream_flatmap} (@ref{4.4.4.6}) to form one
large stream of all the ways that any of the frames in the original input
stream can be extended to produce a match with the given pattern.

@subsubheading Compound queries

@code{And} queries are handled as illustrated in @ref{Figure 4.5}: the frame
stream runs through the conjuncts from left to right, each conjunct extending
the frames the previous ones produced.

@code{Or} queries are handled similarly, as shown in @ref{Figure 4.6}.  The
output streams for the various disjuncts of the @code{or} are computed
separately and merged using the @code{interleave_delayed} procedure from
@ref{4.4.4.6}.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.71} and @ref{Exercise 4.72}.)

@example ocaml
and disjoin session disjuncts frames =
  match disjuncts with
  | [] -> Streams.the_empty_stream
  | first :: rest ->
    interleave_delayed
      (qeval session first frames)
      (fun () -> disjoin session rest frames)
@end example

@subsubheading Filters

@code{Not} is handled by the method outlined in @ref{4.4.2}.  We
attempt to extend each frame in the input stream to satisfy the query being
negated, and we include a given frame in the output stream only if it cannot be
extended.

@code{Holds} is a filter similar to the book's @code{negate}.  Each frame in
the stream is used to instantiate the variables in the terms, the named host
predicate is applied, and the frames for which the predicate returns false are
filtered out of the input stream.  The admitted predicates are @code{<},
@code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=}, @code{=}, and @code{<>} on numbers and
strings.  A @code{Holds} with an unbound variable, an unknown predicate, or an
unknown special form raises @code{Query_error}.  The comparison itself is one
procedure:

@example ocaml
let predicate name args =
  let compare_terms a b =
    match a, b with
    | Num x, Num y -> Int.compare x y
    | Str x, Str y -> String.compare x y
    | _ ->
      fail
        (Eval_error.Type_error
           ("holds " ^ name ^ " compares numbers or strings"))
  in
  match name, args with
  | "<", [ a; b ] -> compare_terms a b < 0
  | ">", [ a; b ] -> compare_terms a b > 0
  | "<=", [ a; b ] -> compare_terms a b <= 0
  | ">=", [ a; b ] -> compare_terms a b >= 0
  | "=", [ a; b ] -> compare_terms a b = 0
  | "<>", [ a; b ] -> compare_terms a b <> 0
  | _ -> fail (Eval_error.Unknown_operation ("holds " ^ name))
;;
@end example

@noindent
The @code{Always_true} query is always satisfied.  It simply passes through
all the frames in the input stream.  @code{Always_true} is the body of rules
like @code{same} that hold for any values of the variables.

@node 4.4.4.3, 4.4.4.4, 4.4.4.2, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Finding Assertions by Pattern Matching

@code{find_assertions}, called by @code{simple_query} (@ref{4.4.4.2}),
takes as input a pattern and a frame.  It returns a stream of frames, each
extending the given one by a data-base match of the given pattern.  It uses
@code{fetch_assertions} (@ref{4.4.4.5}) to get a stream of all the
assertions, and keeps the extensions on which @code{pattern_match} succeeds:

@example ocaml
and find_assertions session pattern frame =
  stream_flatmap
    (fun datum ->
      match pattern_match pattern datum frame with
      | Some extended -> singleton_stream extended
      | None -> Streams.the_empty_stream)
    (fetch_assertions session pattern)
@end example

@noindent
@code{pattern_match} takes a pattern, a variable-free term, and a frame, and
extends the frame so that the pattern matches the term, or answers
@code{None}.  A pattern variable matches anything, consistently with the
frame; two pairs match when both components match; atoms, numbers, strings,
and the empty list match only themselves:

@example ocaml
let rec pattern_match pattern datum frame =
  match pattern, datum with
  | Var v, _ -> extend_if_consistent v datum frame
  | Pair (p1, p2), Pair (d1, d2) ->
    Option.bind (pattern_match p1 d1 frame) (pattern_match p2 d2)
  | Atom a, Atom b when String.equal a b -> Some frame
  | Num a, Num b when a = b -> Some frame
  | Str a, Str b when String.equal a b -> Some frame
  | Nil, Nil -> Some frame
  | _ -> None

and extend_if_consistent v datum frame =
  match binding_in_frame v frame with
  | Some bound -> pattern_match bound datum frame
  | None -> Some (extend v datum frame)
;;
@end example

@subsubheading Patterns with dotted tails

A pattern @code{[computer | ?type]} matches any pair whose head is the atom
@code{computer}, binding @code{?type} to its tail.  The matcher needs no
special case for this: a dotted tail is an ordinary @code{Pair} tail, and the
@code{Pair} clause of @code{pattern_match} walks it like any other pair.
@ref{Exercise 2.20} introduced the dotted-tail notation; here @code{Dotted}
builds it.

@node 4.4.4.4, 4.4.4.5, 4.4.4.3, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Rules and Unification

@code{unify_match} takes two terms, each possibly containing variables, and a
frame, and extends the frame so that the two terms unify, or answers
@code{None}.  Equal variables unify at once; a variable against anything else
extends the frame unless the variable already has a binding, in which case the
binding unifies instead.  Binding a variable to a term that mentions the
variable is refused by the occurs check @code{depends_on}, which follows the
term through the frame:

@example ocaml
let rec unify_match a b frame =
  match a, b with
  | Var v, Var w when same_variable v w -> Some frame
  | Var v, _ -> extend_if_possible v b frame
  | _, Var w -> extend_if_possible w a frame
  | Pair (a1, a2), Pair (b1, b2) ->
    Option.bind (unify_match a1 b1 frame) (unify_match a2 b2)
  | Atom x, Atom y when String.equal x y -> Some frame
  | Num x, Num y when x = y -> Some frame
  | Str x, Str y when String.equal x y -> Some frame
  | Nil, Nil -> Some frame
  | _ -> None

and extend_if_possible v value frame =
  match binding_in_frame v frame, value with
  | Some bound, _ -> unify_match bound value frame
  | None, Var w ->
    (match binding_in_frame w frame with
     | Some bound -> unify_match (Var v) bound frame
     | None -> Some (extend v value frame))
  | None, _ ->
    if depends_on value v frame
    then None
    else Some (extend v value frame)
;;

let rec depends_on t v frame =
  match t with
  | Var w when same_variable v w -> true
  | Var w ->
    (match binding_in_frame w frame with
     | Some bound -> depends_on bound v frame
     | None -> false)
  | Pair (a, b) ->
    depends_on a v frame || depends_on b v frame
  | Atom _ | Num _ | Str _ | Nil -> false
;;
@end example

@noindent
Binding a variable to a term that mentions the variable is refused by the
occurs check @code{depends_on}.@footnote{In general, unifying @code{?y} with an
expression involving @code{?y} would require our being able to find a fixed
point of the equation @code{?y} = @code{<}@var{expression involving @code{?y}}@code{>}.  It
is sometimes possible to syntactically form an expression that appears to be
the solution.  For example, @code{?y} = @code{[f, ?y]} seems to have the fixed
point @code{[f, [f, [f, @r{...}]]]}, which we can produce by beginning with the
expression @code{[f, ?y]} and repeatedly substituting @code{[f, ?y]} for
@code{?y}.  Unfortunately, not every such equation has a meaningful fixed
point.  The issues that arise here are similar to the issues of manipulating
infinite series in mathematics.  For example, we know that 2 is the solution to
the equation @math{{y = 1 + y / 2}}.  Beginning with the expression @math{{1 + y / 2}}
and repeatedly substituting @math{{1 + y / 2}} for @math{y} gives
@displaymath
2 = y = 1 + {y \over 2} = 1 + {{1\over2}\left(1 + {y \over 2}\right)} =
  1 + {1\over2} + {y \over 4} = {\dots,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
2 = y = 1 + y/2 = 1 + (1 + y/2)/2 = 1 + 1/2 + y/4 = ...
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
which leads to
@displaymath
2 = 1 + {1\over2} + {1\over4} + {1\over8} + {\dots.}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo
@example
2 = 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8 + ...
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
However, if we try the same manipulation beginning with the observation that @math{{-1}}
is the solution to the equation @math{{y = 1 + 2y}}, we obtain
@displaymath
{-1} = y = 1 + {2y} = 1 + {2(1 + 2y)} = 1 + 2 + {4y} = {\dots,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
-1 = y = 1 + 2y = 1 + 2(1 + 2y) = 1 + 2 + 4y = ...
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
which leads to
@displaymath
-1 = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + {\dots.}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
-1 = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + ...
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
Although the formal manipulations used in deriving these two equations are
identical, the first result is a valid assertion about infinite series but the
second is not.  Similarly, for our unification results, reasoning with an
arbitrary syntactically constructed expression may lead to errors.}  On the
other hand, we do not want to reject attempts to bind a variable to itself.
For example, consider unifying @code{[?x, ?x]} and @code{[?y, ?y]}.  The second
attempt to bind @code{?x} to @code{?y} matches @code{?y} (the stored value of
@code{?x}) against @code{?y} (the new value of @code{?x}).  This is taken care
of by the @code{Var} clauses of @code{unify_match}.



@noindent
To apply a rule, the query pattern is unified with the rule's conclusion after
renaming every variable of the rule to the current rule-application id, so
that two applications of one rule never share a variable.  The rule body is
then evaluated in the unified frame:

@example ocaml
and apply_rules session pattern frame =
  stream_flatmap
    (fun rule -> apply_a_rule session rule pattern frame)
    (fetch_rules session pattern)

and apply_a_rule session rule pattern frame =
  let conclusion, body =
    rename_variables_in rule (new_rule_application_id session)
  in
  match unify_match pattern conclusion frame with
  | Some unified -> qeval session body (singleton_stream unified)
  | None -> Streams.the_empty_stream
;;

let rename_variables_in (conclusion, body) id =
  let rec rename = function
    | Var v -> Var @{ v with id @}
    | Pair (a, b) -> Pair (rename a, rename b)
    | (Atom _ | Num _ | Str _ | Nil) as t -> t
  in
  rename conclusion, map_terms rename body
;;
@end example

@node 4.4.4.5, 4.4.4.6, 4.4.4.4, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Maintaining the Data Base

One important problem in designing logic programming languages is that of
arranging things so that as few irrelevant data-base entries as possible will
be examined in checking a given pattern.  This edition keeps the design
simple: a session holds its assertions and rules in insertion order in two
lists, and fetching returns every entry.  The row order of the transcripts of
@ref{4.4.1} reproduces the order in which the assertions were added, because
@code{add_assertion} appends each term after the earlier ones:

@example ocaml
type session =
  @{ mutable assertions : term list
  ; mutable rules : (term * query) list
  ; mutable counter : int
  ; handlers : (string, handler) Hashtbl.t
  @}

and handler =
  session
  -> query list
  -> frame Streams.stream
  -> frame Streams.stream

let new_session () =
  @{ assertions = []
  ; rules = []
  ; counter = 0
  ; handlers = Hashtbl.create 8
  @}
;;

let put session name handler =
  Hashtbl.replace session.handlers name handler
;;

let add_assertion session t =
  session.assertions <- session.assertions @@ [ t ]
;;

let add_rule session conclusion body =
  session.rules <- session.rules @@ [ conclusion, body ]
;;

let fetch_assertions session _pattern =
  list_to_stream session.assertions
;;

let fetch_rules session _pattern = list_to_stream session.rules

let new_rule_application_id session =
  session.counter <- session.counter + 1;
  session.counter
;;
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.70}Exercise 4.70:} The book's original data base
held its assertions in a stream whose tail was delayed, and
@code{add-assertion!} snapshotted the old stream in a @code{let} before
extending it, so that the delayed tail closed over the old value.  What goes
wrong with a version that extends the stream without the snapshot, forcing
the tail only after the assignment?  Hint: recall the infinite stream of ones
in @ref{3.5.2}.

@enumerate a

@item
This edition stores its assertions in the list field @code{assertions}, and
@code{add_assertion} assigns @code{session.assertions @@ [t]}.  The right-hand
side is evaluated before the field is updated: the same old-value discipline,
with no laziness for the hazard to hide behind.  Where did the hazard go?

@item
The transcripts of @ref{4.4.1} reproduce insertion order.  What would change
in them if @code{add_assertion} consed each term at the front instead of
appending it at the back?

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@node 4.4.4.6, 4.4.4.7, 4.4.4.5, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Stream Operations

The streams of frames are the memoized streams of @ref{3.5}.  Combining two
streams delays the second one: @code{stream_append_delayed} walks the first
stream and only then runs the delayed computation, and
@code{interleave_delayed} alternates the two, which keeps a rule-derived
stream from starving the streams behind it.  Flattening maps each frame to
its stream of extensions and interleaves the results, so one frame's infinite
extensions cannot hide the frames behind it:

@example ocaml
let singleton_stream x =
  Streams.cons_stream x (fun () -> Streams.the_empty_stream)
;;

let rec stream_append_delayed s1 delayed_s2 =
  match s1 with
  | Streams.Empty -> delayed_s2 ()
  | Streams.Cons (head, tail) ->
    Streams.cons_stream head (fun () ->
      stream_append_delayed (Lazy.force tail)
        delayed_s2)
;;

let rec interleave_delayed s1 delayed_s2 =
  match s1 with
  | Streams.Empty -> delayed_s2 ()
  | Streams.Cons (head, tail) ->
    Streams.cons_stream head (fun () ->
      interleave_delayed (delayed_s2 ())
        (fun () -> Lazy.force tail))
;;

let rec flatten_stream = function
  | Streams.Empty -> Streams.the_empty_stream
  | Streams.Cons (head, tail) ->
    interleave_delayed head
      (fun () -> flatten_stream (Lazy.force tail))
;;

let stream_flatmap f s = flatten_stream (Streams.stream_map f s)

let rec list_to_stream = function
  | [] -> Streams.the_empty_stream
  | x :: rest -> Streams.cons_stream x (fun () -> list_to_stream rest)
;;
@end example

@node 4.4.4.7, 4.4.4.8, 4.4.4.6, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Query Syntax Procedures

Queries reach the evaluator as constructor values, never as text.  The one
place that reads a surface syntax is the fixture decoder: @code{read_fixture}
decodes a constructor-literal program, a list of @code{Assert},
@code{Rule}, and @code{Query} commands with terms built from @code{Atom},
@code{Num}, @code{Str}, @code{Var}, @code{List}, and @code{Dotted}, which is
the shape of every session of @ref{4.4.1}:

@example ocaml
let rec decode_term = function
  | Data.Ctor ("Atom", [ Data.String a ]) -> Ok (Atom a)
  | Data.Ctor ("Num", [ Data.Int n ]) -> Ok (Num n)
  | Data.Ctor ("Str", [ Data.String s ]) -> Ok (Str s)
  | Data.Ctor ("Var", [ Data.String v ]) -> Ok (var v)
  | Data.Ctor ("List", [ Data.List items ]) ->
    Result.map list (each decode_term items)
  | Data.Ctor ("Dotted", [ Data.List items; tail ]) ->
    let* items = each decode_term items in
    let* tail = decode_term tail in
    Ok (dotted items tail)
  | d -> wanted "a term (Atom, Num, Str, Var, List, Dotted)" d
;;

let rec decode_query = function
  | Data.Ctor ("Pattern", [ t ]) ->
    Result.map (fun t -> Pattern t) (decode_term t)
  | Data.Ctor ("And", [ Data.List qs ]) ->
    Result.map (fun qs -> And qs) (each decode_query qs)
  | Data.Ctor ("Or", [ Data.List qs ]) ->
    Result.map (fun qs -> Or qs) (each decode_query qs)
  | Data.Ctor ("Not", [ q ]) ->
    Result.map (fun q -> Not q) (decode_query q)
  | Data.Ctor ("Holds", [ Data.String p; Data.List ts ]) ->
    Result.map (fun ts -> Holds (p, ts)) (each decode_term ts)
  | Data.Ctor ("Always_true", []) -> Ok Always_true
  | d ->
    wanted "a query (Pattern, And, Or, Not, Holds, Always_true)" d
;;
@end example

@noindent
The entry point ties the two levels together: it reads constructor data and
decodes the command list:

@example ocaml
let read_fixture ~filename text =
  let* d = Data.read ~filename text in
  match d with
  | Data.List commands -> each decode_command commands
  | d -> wanted "a list of commands" d
;;
@end example

@noindent
Queries reach the evaluator as constructor values, so there is no reader to
extend with new surface syntax in this edition.  The decoder above is the
whole syntax layer, written out explicitly.@footnote{Most Lisp systems give
the user the ability to modify the ordinary reader to perform such
transformations by defining @newterm{reader macro characters}.  This edition
needs no such machinery: constructor data enters through @code{Data.read},
and the decoder turns it into terms and queries directly.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.71}Exercise 4.71:} Louis Reasoner wonders why the
@code{simple_query} and @code{disjoin} procedures (@ref{4.4.4.2}) are
implemented using explicitly delayed streams, rather than being defined
as follows:

@example ocaml
let simple_query session query_pattern frame =
  stream_flatmap
    (fun frame ->
       Streams.stream_append
         (find_assertions session query_pattern frame)
         (apply_rules session query_pattern frame))
    (singleton_stream frame)

let rec disjoin session disjuncts frames =
  match disjuncts with
  | [] -> Streams.the_empty_stream
  | first :: rest ->
    Streams.interleave
      (qeval session first frames)
      (disjoin session rest frames)
@end example

Can you give examples of queries where these simpler definitions would lead to
undesirable behavior?
@end quotation

